<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Béla Bartók</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Béla Viktor János Bartók, Bartók</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Béla Bartók was a Hungarian composer, pianist and ethnomusicologist, born in 1881 in Nagyszentmiklós in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, now Sânnicolau Mare in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He studied at the Royal Academy of Music in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoltan-kodaly">Zoltán Kodály</a>, and after hearing Lidi Dósa sing folk songs in 1904 he devoted himself to folk music, whose collection and study made him one of the founders of comparative musicology, later known as ethnomusicology. His works include the opera Bluebeard's Castle, the ballet The Miraculous Mandarin, the Concerto for Orchestra and six string quartets. He emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in October 1940 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City on 26 September 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bela-bartok.jpg" alt="Béla Bartók" width="330" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartók_Béla_1927.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bartók's father, the director of an agricultural school, died suddenly in 1888, when the boy was seven. His mother, Paula, then took Béla and his sister Erzsébet to live in Nagyszőlős and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>. According to her, he could tell dance rhythms apart before he spoke in full sentences, and by the age of four he could play 40 pieces on the piano. He gave his first public recital at 11 in Nagyszőlős, including his own first composition, &quot;The Course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1899 to 1903 he studied piano under István Thomán and composition under János Koessler. In 1902 he heard Richard Strauss's Also sprach Zarathustra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, which he said showed him the way that lay before him, and in 1903 he wrote the symphonic poem Kossuth. After 1907 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-debussy">Claude Debussy</a>'s music influenced him, and he began teaching piano at the Liszt Academy of Music, which freed him from touring as a pianist.</p><p>He married Márta Ziegler in 1909 and, after their divorce in June 1923, Ditta Pásztory. He wrote Bluebeard's Castle in 1911; a commission rejected it as unfit for the stage, and he revised it for its 1918 première. After 1933 he refused to give concerts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in October 1940 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, settling in New York City. He became a US citizen in 1945, but felt never fully at home there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His music reflects the breakdown of the diatonic system of harmony and the revival of nationalism as a source of inspiration. He collected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> peasant melodies and later those of Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusyns">Rusyns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Croatians, and drew also on the folk music of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. He described three ways in which peasant music may enter modern music: taking a melody over, inventing imitations, or absorbing its idiom as a mother tongue.</p><p>Among his main works are the six string quartets (1909 to 1939), the Cantata Profana (1930), which he called his most personal credo, the Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta (1936), the Concerto for Orchestra (1943) and the Third Piano Concerto (1945). For his son Péter's lessons he composed Mikrokosmos, a six-volume collection of graded piano pieces. His works entered the public domain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on 1 January 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Béla Bartók” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
